# Line endings are pinned to LF, in the repository AND in the working tree.
#
# WHY eol=lf AND NOT THE WINDOWS DEFAULT. Git on Windows defaults to
# core.autocrlf=true: store LF, check out CRLF. Without this file the whole
# tree would be rewritten the next time Git touched it and every one of the 234
# source files would show as modified straight after checkout.
#
# MEASURED, because the tree turned out to have MIXED endings: 54 of the 234
# files under src/ carry CRLF today and 180 are LF. Normalising is therefore
# not a no-op — it removes 15 627 carriage returns, so a fresh clone builds an
# artifact about 15.6 KB smaller than the one measured on the machine this was
# committed from. Nothing about the app changes; a CR is whitespace between
# statements. But it does mean **the byte count in MASTER-PLAN Part 0 is a
# measurement of one working tree, not a constant** — run ./measure.ps1 rather
# than trusting it, which is what Part 0 says anyway.
#
# LF is the direction chosen because it is what the majority of the tree
# already uses and what every non-Windows checkout would get regardless.
# PowerShell 5.1 reads LF-only .ps1 files perfectly well; what it does NOT
# tolerate is a missing UTF-8 BOM on a script carrying Unicode in its patterns
# (see MASTER-PLAN 1.6) — and `text` handling never touches the BOM, so that
# convention is unaffected.

* text=auto eol=lf

# Binary: never touch these, never try to diff them as text.
*.png  binary
*.jpg  binary
*.ico  binary
*.pdf  binary
